# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 5 is syllabus **Head VI — Status of Women in Pakistan**. The syllabus names four lines: women's **health**, **education**, **employment** and **law**. The examiner has added three newer ones: Pakistan's **gender-gap ranking** (2018, 2026), **intersectional** disadvantage (2026) and the **digital gender gap** (2026). This document teaches all seven, and adds women's **political** status and **climate** impacts in brief.

In the descriptive paper Topic 5 has had ==8.5 visits==: eight full questions and one short note. **Health** is the favourite line (2017, 2023, 2024); **education** has never been a full question — the clearest blind spot in the paper. The topic also supplies **19 of the 140 MCQs**, mostly on laws and dates: MFLO ==1961==, the harassment law ==2010==, the Women's Protection Act ==2006==, the NCSW ==2000==.

This topic **feeds every other**. Whenever you need a figure, a law or an institution for an answer on development (Topic 4), governance (Topic 6) or violence (Topic 7), it is here.

::: simple
"**Status**" means a person's position in society — what rights, resources, respect and power she has compared with others. To judge women's status in Pakistan, ask four questions: **Is she healthy? Is she educated? Can she earn? Does the law protect her — in practice, not only on paper?**
:::

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic, the four dimensions of status, and the constitutional framework.
2. **Part One** has ten chapters: measuring status (1); health and maternal health (2–3); education (4); employment (5); the Constitution and family law (6); protective laws, human rights and the enforcement gap (7); intersectional disadvantage (8); the digital gender gap (9); and political status and climate (10).
3. **Part Two** sets out today's debates.
4. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
5. **Part Four** is a bank of about 120 one-liners, followed by every past MCQ on the topic.
6. **Part Five** is the revision sheet.

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 5

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 5 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.6,10.4
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Status of women ★ | Women's position in society — rights, resources, respect and power — compared with men's.
Global Gender Gap Index ★ | The World Economic Forum's yearly ranking of countries on **economic participation, education, health and political empowerment** (since ==2006==).
Maternal mortality ratio (MMR) ★ | Deaths of women from pregnancy or childbirth per **100,000 live births**. Pakistan: ==186== (2019).
Antenatal care | Health check-ups during pregnancy.
Skilled birth attendant | A trained doctor, nurse or midwife who helps at childbirth.
Anaemia | Lack of healthy red blood cells, often from iron deficiency; common among pregnant women.
Stunting | Children too short for their age because of long-term undernutrition.
Total fertility rate | The average number of children a woman has in her lifetime.
Lady Health Worker (LHW) ★ | A community health worker who provides basic health and family-planning care door to door (programme from ==1994==).
Literacy rate ★ | The share of people who can read and write. Census 2023: about ==68%== men, ==53%== women.
Out-of-school children | Children of school age not enrolled — more than 25 million in Pakistan, most of them girls.
Labour-force participation rate (LFPR) ★ | The share of working-age people who work or seek work. Women: ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25).
Informal sector | Work without legal protection or social security — home-based, domestic, unpaid family work.
Double burden ★ | Paid work plus unpaid housework and care — the "second shift".
Glass ceiling ★ | Invisible barriers that stop women rising to the top (MCQ 2024).
Sticky floor | Women stuck in **low-wage, low-mobility** jobs (MCQ 2026).
Comparable worth | Equal pay for work of **equal value**; its absence means women's jobs are paid less (MCQ 2023).
Gender pay gap | The difference between men's and women's average earnings.
Fundamental rights | Rights guaranteed in the Constitution (Articles 8–28), enforceable in court.
Principles of Policy | Goals the state should pursue (Articles 29–40), not directly enforceable.
Muslim Family Laws Ordinance ★ | The ==1961== law on marriage registration, polygamy, divorce and orphaned grandchildren's inheritance.
*Khula* | Divorce sought by the wife through a court.
*Haq mehr* | The marriage gift (dower) owed by the husband to the wife.
Enforcement gap ★ | The distance between laws on paper and their application in practice.
NCSW ★ | **National Commission on the Status of Women**, set up in ==2000== under Pervez Musharraf.
Intersectionality ★ | Overlapping identities — gender, class, place, religion, disability — that create unique disadvantage.
Digital gender gap ★ | The difference between men's and women's access to and use of phones and the internet.
Reserved seats | Seats for women in legislatures: ==60== in the National Assembly.
:::

## 0.2  The four dimensions of women's status

::: fig grid | The four syllabus dimensions of women's status, with Pakistan's headline figure.
@cols 4
Health: maternal mortality 186 per 100,000 live births (2019)
Education: female literacy about 53% (Census 2023)
Employment: female labour-force participation 24.4% (2024–25)
Law: strong laws, weak enforcement
:::

## 0.3  The constitutional framework

::: law Constitution of Pakistan 1973 | the articles on women
- **Article 25:** all citizens are equal before law; **no discrimination on the basis of sex**; the state may make **special provision for women and children**.
- **Article 25A:** free and compulsory education for children aged **5 to 16** (added by the 18th Amendment, ==2010==).
- **Articles 26–27:** no discrimination in access to public places or in public service on the basis of sex.
- **Article 32:** representation of **women** in local government.
- **Article 34:** steps to ensure **full participation of women** in all spheres of national life.
- **Article 35:** the state shall protect the **marriage, the family, the mother and the child**.
- **Article 37(e):** just and humane conditions of work, and **maternity benefits** for women in employment.
- **Articles 51, 59, 106:** **reserved seats** for women in the National Assembly, Senate and provincial assemblies.
:::

::: eye | How the examiner uses this topic
The examiner asks you to **describe** women's status and then to **explain** and **improve** it: "How could it be improved within the available economic resources?" (2017); "What reasons are responsible…?" (2018); "What measures could be taken?" (2023); "Identify the factors…" (2026). A status answer without **causes and solutions** is half an answer. Learn, for each dimension: (1) the **latest data**; (2) the **causes**; (3) the **laws and programmes**; (4) the **way forward**.
:::
